6.text:
For a just identified model put

\begin{equation*}
x_{i}=\pi z_{i}+v_{i},\qquad y_{i}=\beta x_{i}+u_{i},\qquad 
{\rm E}z_{i}u_{i}={\rm E}z_{i}v_{i}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and write
$\hat\pi_{x}= (Z'X)/(Z'Z)$ and $\hat\pi_{y}= (Z'Y)/(Z'Z)$.
With one instrument

\begin{equation*}
\hat\beta_{2SLS}= \hat\pi_{y}/\hat\pi_{x}
          =\beta+\frac{\hat\pi_{x}\hat\pi_{y}-\pi\beta\pi}
                 {\hat\pi_{x}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the weak–instrument local-to-zero sequence
$\pi_{n}=c/\sqrt n$ the Nagar first order bias is

\begin{equation*}
E\big[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]\approx
\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\,
\frac{k-2}{\mu^{2}},\qquad 
\mu^{2}=n\pi^{2}Q_{zz}/\sigma_{v}^{2},
\end{equation*}
and $k=1$.  Up to the $O_{p}(1)$ OLS bias constant the
asymptotic relative bias is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\frac{{\rm Bias}\big(\hat\beta_{2SLS}\big)}
     {{\rm Bias}\big(\hat\beta_{OLS}\big)}
\approx \frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  in practice
${\rm RB}\approx 1/(F-1)\approx1/F$.
Put $\hat\mu^{2}=F-1$.  In the weak instrument limit
$F\;\xrightarrow{d}\;(\,Z+\sqrt\mu^{2}\,)^{2}$,
$Z\sim N(0,1)$, so that with the usual normal approximation

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt F =\sqrt{\mu^{2}}+Z+o_{p}(1) .
\end{equation*}
A one-sided $95\%$ lower confidence bound for $\sqrt{\mu^{2}}$
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{\mu^{2}}_{L}= \sqrt F -z_{0.95} ,
\qquad z_{0.95}=1.645 ,
\end{equation*}
and, up to the $+1$, $\mu_{L}^{2}\approx\big(\sqrt F-z_{0.95}\big)^{2}$.
Requiring the 95\% lower bound for the Nagar relative bias to be
$<0.10$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1}{\mu_{L}^{2}}<0.10\Longleftrightarrow
\mu_{L}^{2}>10,
\end{equation*}
gives the familiar first-order inversion

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt F> \sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\approx 3.162+1.645=4.81,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}\approx\big(\sqrt{10}+1.645\big)^{2}\approx 2.3\!-\!2.4\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}
about $23$–$24$, $F\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx 26$ on the
usual two-sided $95\%$ normalisation.  In particular
$\sqrt{\mu^{2}}_{L}= \sqrt F-1.645> \sqrt{10}$ is the weak–instrument
form of the Stock–Yogo $1/F$ rule.  Ignoring the sampling
uncertainty in $F$ the first order Nagar rule is simply
${\rm RB}\approx1/F<0.10\iff F>10$, the textbook $F>10$ rule of thumb,
which is often quoted as the $F\approx 8.96\!-\!16.4$ Stock–Yogo
weak-instrument critical value, e.g. the conventional $F\approx10$
$ \approx 9$–$16$ in the tables.  This is the $F\approx10$ rule quoted
in the literature; with the $95\%$ normal margin it becomes the
$F\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.645)^{2}\approx23$ conservative bound, often
rounded to $ \sim 24\!-\!26$ in practice.

